libgnushim — copyright and licensing
====================================

libgnushim is licensed **GPL-3.0-or-later**. The full text is in `LICENSE` (verbatim GPLv3, not
transcribed).

Why a separate note, when there is already a LICENSE
----------------------------------------------------
This library is not wholly original work, and saying only "GPLv3+" would obscure where the code
came from. Three different provenances live in this repository:

1. **`testdir/` — generated GNU gnulib source.**
   Produced by `regenerate.sh` from a pinned gnulib checkout (37b6f12946, 2020-08-01) and committed
   so the build is reproducible without network access. These files are **gnulib's, under gnulib's
   own terms**, and each carries its own header — read the file, not this note. gnulib modules are
   variously GPL-3.0-or-later and LGPL-2.1-or-later; the combined static library as distributed
   here is therefore GPL-3.0-or-later, which is compatible with both.

2. **`aliases.c`, `getopt_long.c`, `gnushim.h`, `probe/`, and the build scripts — original work**
   for this port, copyright Hugo Hurskainen, GPL-3.0-or-later.
   `getopt_long.c` exists precisely because gnulib's getopt carries global state that the alias
   layer cannot passthrough; it is an independent implementation, not a gnulib copy.

3. **`modules.txt`** — a list of gnulib module names. A list of names, not code.

What this means for a consumer
------------------------------
Linking `-lgnushim` into a program links gnulib-derived code, so the resulting binary inherits
those obligations. That is the normal situation for anything that uses gnulib, and it is the reason
the shim exists as a separate, clearly-labelled library rather than being copied ad hoc into each
port: one place to look, one licence to reason about.

Documentation in this repository is GPL-3.0-or-later. Hugo's to relicense if he would rather it
were permissive.
